WEBVTT

00:00:00.023 --> 00:00:02.159
You send the exact same prompt twice.

00:00:02.354 --> 00:00:07.491
On the second request, LMCache finds all two hundred and fifty-seven tokens.

00:00:07.696 --> 00:00:12.320
But vLLM plans to load only two hundred and fifty-six.

00:00:12.525 --> 00:00:15.202
Why leave one out?

00:00:15.394 --> 00:00:18.557
Start with how a model generates its first output token.

00:00:18.749 --> 00:00:22.091
The model splits your text into tokens,

00:00:22.091 --> 00:00:25.517
then processes the input. That's prefill.

00:00:25.709 --> 00:00:31.109
At the last input position, the model produces a score for every candidate in its vocabulary.

00:00:31.302 --> 00:00:33.925
Those scores are called logits.

00:00:34.131 --> 00:00:39.702
vLLM uses them to pick the first output token.

00:00:39.907 --> 00:00:42.504
The KV cache holds a different piece of the computation.

00:00:42.696 --> 00:00:46.162
Think of it as notes that each attention layer takes while reading the prompt.

00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:51.123
At a new position, the layer can look back at those notes.

00:00:51.328 --> 00:00:56.623
If a new request starts with the same tokens, the model can reuse the notes and skip that earlier work.

00:00:56.828 --> 00:01:00.899
The notes don't contain the logits.

00:01:01.104 --> 00:01:07.096
To get those, the model can run the last prompt token again, using the earlier KV.

00:01:07.302 --> 00:01:09.675
Now let's repeat a request.

00:01:09.881 --> 00:01:14.407
The first time, LMCache saves one chunk of two hundred and fifty-six tokens

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:17.438
and a one-token tail.

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:23.820
Then we clear just vLLM's local prefix cache and send the same prompt again.

00:01:24.025 --> 00:01:30.373
The local cache misses. LMCache still has all two hundred and fifty-seven tokens.

00:01:30.565 --> 00:01:36.123
The plan is to load the first two hundred and fifty-six and run the last one in the next forward pass.

00:01:36.328 --> 00:01:37.965
Here's where the lengths get out of sync.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:44.833
The scheduler gives the load enough GPU blocks for one full chunk.

00:01:45.025 --> 00:01:49.030
But retrieve still looks up objects for the original two hundred and fifty-seven tokens.

00:01:49.236 --> 00:01:51.544
The full chunk has a destination.

00:01:51.749 --> 00:01:56.846
The tail has none.

00:01:57.038 --> 00:02:03.846
When the tail handler asks for a GPU block, it throws, and the whole retrieval fails.

00:02:04.038 --> 00:02:07.491
The pull request adds a check inside retrieve.

00:02:07.696 --> 00:02:11.617
If the tail object exists and every group has an empty list of destination blocks for it,

00:02:11.617 --> 00:02:15.215
skip the tail and keep the full chunks.

00:02:15.407 --> 00:02:20.215
If some groups have blocks and others don't, the existing check still raises.

00:02:20.407 --> 00:02:24.702
The token count also leaves out the skipped tail.

00:02:24.907 --> 00:02:28.741
So when you see a full hit, ask three questions.

00:02:28.946 --> 00:02:30.254
How much was stored?

00:02:30.459 --> 00:02:32.070
How much are we loading?

00:02:32.275 --> 00:02:33.688
And what still runs?

00:02:33.881 --> 00:02:39.386
Here it's two hundred and fifty-seven, two hundred and fifty-six, and that very last token.

00:02:39.591 --> 00:02:47.168
Once the load succeeds, the forward pass produces the logits and generation can begin.
